{
  "summary": "The existing ideation has converged on a strong design after 12 rounds of debate. The core architecture is sound: immutable 8-char hex IDs as dependency references, two module-level functions in state.py (resolve_dependencies and detect_cycle) as the single source of truth, blocking enforced only in auto-mode's impl_stages pass with topological sorting, manual moves always permitted with warning logging and visible override indicators, plan_invalidation_check on rejection moving dependents to plan-inbox, dependency_events as an append-only audit trail with per-event 'addressed' marking, conditional JSON persistence (omit when empty), and a _dependency_dirty cache flag on PipelineServer. The design correctly decouples dependency resolution from plan validity, routes all mutations through _dispatch_command, and keeps blocked-status computation server-side in Python. Three areas deserve final attention for implementation readiness: (1) the dependency_events field needs a concrete schema definition beyond the example entry — specifically, what fields are required vs optional, what event types exist beyond 'dep_rejected', and how the planning prompt in phases.py should format unaddressed events for LLM consumption; (2) the Go FeatureSummary struct additions (DependsOn []string, Blocked bool, Override bool) need corresponding updates in _build_golang_state_message() and the WebUI JavaScript that consumes the state — the blocked/override rendering logic in the kanban cards needs to handle the three-state display (normal, blocked with red lock, overridden with yellow warning); (3) the detect_cycle function must handle the edge case where a feature's depends_on contains its own ID (self-reference), which is technically not a cycle in the graph-theoretic sense but should be rejected as invalid input before cycle detection runs."
}
